vice deployment is not in a ready state (0/1), I need to examine detailed deployment events and container status. This will reveal potential issues like image pull failures, startup errors, resource constraints, or configuration problems preventing the pod from starting successfully.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 352, 'prompt_tokens': 2434, 'total_tokens': 2786, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcf0-1a02-7223-9ac1-031000f90342-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2434, 'output_tokens': 352, 'total_tokens': 2786}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='28ff00b1-ce3c-45c4-aa86-b01cb1fae60d'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service deployment is showing 0/1 READY, which means the pod isn't running. The previous step was using kubectl describe on the deployment to get more details. The response from that command will show the events and the current state of the pod. I need to check if there are any errors in the events, like failed pulls, crashes, or resource issues. Also, looking at the container status might indicate why it's not starting. Once I get that info, I can determine the root cause, like an image issue or misconfiguration. Then, I can use the submit_tool to report the findings.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93d1c212792dd27d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'com