ents" and saw the user-service deployment with 0 available. The new replica set is user-service-746f96d8d6, but the pod name in the error is user-service-746f96d8d6-654321. Maybe the pod hasn\'t been assigned that name yet. Maybe they should check the pods in the namespace to see if any pods are in the process of being created. \n\nSo the next step would be to list all the pods in the social-network namespace to see if the user-service pod is present. If not, maybe there\'s a scheduling issue. Alternatively, check the events in the deployment for more details. The user can use "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to see the current state. If the pod is not there, then the problem might be with the deployment configuration, such as resource limits, node selectors, or image pull issues. \n\nAlternatively, using the get_metrics tool to check if the user-service is up might help, but earlier attempts showed that the up query returned empty. So maybe the service isn\'t running. The user should first confirm if the pod is actually being created. If not, check the deployment\'s events and the pod\'s status. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to get the list of pods in the social-network namespace to see if the user-service pod is present. If it\'s not there, then the deployment might be failing to create the pod, which could be due to various reasons like image pull errors, resource constraints, or configuration issues.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo verify if the user-service pod is actually being created, I need to check the current list of pods in the social-network namespace. The previous attempt to access logs failed because the pod might not exist yet, indicating a potential issue with deployment or scheduling. This command will confirm whether the pod is in the process of being created or if there are errors preventing its creation.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal':