746f96d8d6-654321. Maybe the pod hasn\'t been assigned that name yet. Maybe they should check the pods in the namespace to see if any pods are in the process of being created. \n\nSo the next step would be to list all the pods in the social-network namespace to see if the user-service pod is present. If not, maybe there\'s a scheduling issue. Alternatively, check the events in the deployment for more details. The user can use "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to see the current state. If the pod is not there, then the problem might be with the deployment configuration, such as resource limits, node selectors, or image pull issues. \n\nAlternatively, using the get_metrics tool to check if the user-service is up might help, but earlier attempts showed that the up query returned empty. So maybe the service isn\'t running. The user should first confirm if the pod is actually being created. If not, check the deployment\'s events and the pod\'s status. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to get the list of pods in the social-network namespace to see if the user-service pod is present. If it\'s not there, then the deployment might be failing to create the pod, which could be due to various reasons like image pull errors, resource constraints, or configuration issues.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo verify if the user-service pod is actually being created, I need to check the current list of pods in the social-network namespace. The previous attempt to access logs failed because the pod might not exist yet, indicating a potential issue with deployment or scheduling. This command will confirm whether the pod is in the process of being created or if there are errors preventing its creation.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 744, 'prompt_tokens': 3373, 'total_tokens': 4117, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcf0-5958-7db1-a2be-b2b464bd0a31-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3373, 'output_tokens': 744, 'total_tokens': 4117}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b4dbe243-e232-4756-8c43-6583269da894'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in their social network application isn\'t running properly. They\'ve already checked the deployments and saw that the user-service deployment has 0 available replicas, which means the pod isn\'t up. They tried to describe the deployment and got some info, but when they tried to check the pod logs, the pod wasn\'t found. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the current state of the pods again. The user ran "kubectl get pods -n social-network" earlier, and the output showed that the user-service pod was listed as 0/1, which means it\'s in a pending or crashed state. But when they tried to access the logs, the pod wasn\'t found. That could mean the pod was created but immediately failed, or maybe it\'s still in the process of being scheduled.\n\nWait, the user just tried to describe the pod with a specific name, but the system said it wasn\'t found. That suggests the pod might not have been created yet, or maybe the name in the deployment is different. The deployment\'s pod template might have a different name or the pod is still being scheduled. \n\nSo, the best approach is to run "kubectl get pods -n social-network" again to see the current list. If the pod is still not there, it could be a problem with the deployment configuration, like image pull failures, resource limits, or scheduling constraints. Alternatively, if the pod is listed but in a CrashLoopBackOff state, that would indicate an application error. \n\nBy checking the pods again, the user can confirm if the pod is being created and its current status. If the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, they can then look into the logs or check the events for more details. If the pod isn\'t listed, the issue might be with the deployment configuration or the node where it\'s supposed to run. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the list of pods again to verify their current state and identify any issues with the user-service pod.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bdd097d5a88b722e', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 465, 'prompt_tokens': 4369, 'total_tokens': 4834, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcf0-7bd4-7e90-930b-e20ca52b1737-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bdd097d5a88b722e', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4369, 'output_tokens': 465, 'total_tokens': 4834}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\ncompose-post-service-886c98b48-bd7px      1/1     Running   0          2m38s\nhome-timeline-redis-5cdbd67f7c-8npbr      1/1     Running   0          2m38s\nhome-timeline-service-5d6bb5597d-kkqlk    1/1     Running   0          2m36s\njaeger-68c668dfbc-qlzhd                   1/1     Running   0          2m36s\nmedia-frontend-f49684d4c-mqbfs            1/1     Running   0          2m35s\nmedia-memcached-8bdff88f6-rj228           1/1