a={}, id='9232ff14-035d-407c-934b-92571b487bbf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is in a "Running" state but with 0/1 containers ready. The previous attempt to get logs failed because the pod name might have changed or there was an error. Now that I know the pod is named user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf, I should try getting its logs again. The logs will show any errors during startup. Also, checking the readiness probe configuration is important because if the probe is failing, the pod won\'t be marked as ready. The readiness probe is set to check http://:8080/healthz, so maybe the application isn\'t starting correctly or the health check endpoint is wrong. I should look into the logs to find out why the health check isn\'t passing. Another possibility is resource constraints, but since the pod is running, maybe it\'s a configuration issue. Let me check the logs first.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf -n social-network"  \nThe user-service pod is in the "Running" state but not marked as ready. To diagnose the issue, I need to inspect the container logs to identify runtime errors, failed startup processes, or issues with the readiness probe (e.g., incorrect health check endpoint, application crashes, or configuration misloads). This will directly reveal the root cause of the pod\'s unavailability.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 303, 'prompt_tokens': 4540, 'total_tokens': 4843, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcf0-941d-7c91-bff4-741bf05f92ac-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4540, 'output_tokens': 303, 'total_tokens': 4843}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a9785b69-dcff-4aeb-bfd2-5f5b9711c2d6'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is listed as Running but not Ready. The previous attempt to get logs failed because the pod name was incorrect. Now, the correct pod name is user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf. So, I need to check the logs for this pod.\n\nThe next step is to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the command "kubectl logs user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf -n social-network". This will retrieve the logs from the container, which can help identify any errors during startup or runtime. Common issues might include application crashes, configuration errors, or problems with the readiness probe. By examining the logs, I can pinpoint the exact cause of the pod not being ready. If the logs show an error related to the readiness probe, maybe the health check endpoint is incorrect. Alternatively, there could be an issue with the application itself, like a missing dependency or a misconfigured environment variable. The logs should provide the necessary clues to determine the root cause.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-856dd3320988cb27', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 269, 'prompt_tokens': 5552, 'total_tokens': 5821, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcf0-a2b5-79f1-b93d-0667a99b19a9-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-856dd3320988cb27', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5552, 'output_tokens': 269, 'total_tokens': 5821}), ToolMessage(content='[2026-Mar-17 17:54:49.150718] <info>: (UserHandler.h:932:GetMachineId) MAC address = 36:d5:74:ae:7d:0f\n[2026-Mar-17 17:54:49.150905] <info>: (UserService.cpp:63:main) machine_id = 94f\n[2026-Mar-17 17:54:49.156875] <info>: (UserService.cpp:94:main) Starting the user-service server ...\n', id='55c98121-9d02-48b2-81ad-0a32a4cfcc91', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-856dd3320988cb27'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='00ad420a-93ad-4cc0-901c-0e7ccd08e227'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is running but not ready. The logs show the service starting up, but there\'s no indication of an error. The readiness probe might be the issue. The previous attempt to get logs failed because the pod wasn\'t found, but now we have the logs. The logs don\'t show any errors, so maybe the problem is with the readiness probe configuration. The deployment\'s readiness probe is set to http-get http://:8080/healthz. Wait, the container might be listening on a different port or the health check endpoint is incorrect. Let me check the pod\'s container ports.\n\nI should use kubectl describe pod to see the container\'s ports and check if 8080 is exposed. Also, verify if the readiness probe\'s port matches the container\'s exposed ports. If the probe is using port 8080 but the container is actually using a different port, that would cause the probe to fail, leading to the pod not being marked as ready. Let me check the pod\'s details.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe pod user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf -n social-network"  \nTo verify the container\'s port configuration and readiness probe settings, I need to inspect the pod\'s detailed specifications. This will confirm whether the readiness probe\'s endpoint (`http://:8080/healthz`) aligns with the actual port the container is listening on. Mismatched ports or incorrect health check endpoints can prevent the pod from transitioning to a ready state, even if the application is running.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 337, 'prompt_tokens': 4902, 'total_tokens': 5239, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcf0-b29c-7170-b3dc-7bd9ef27f0da-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4902, 'output_tokens': 337, 'total_tokens': 5239}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='eae7e2eb-d38f-4a06-9f74-d2a0bb2a8d91'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service pod is running but not ready. The logs show the service started, but the readiness probe might be failing. The previous logs didn't show any errors, so maybe the issue is with the readiness probe configuration.\n\nI need to check the pod's description to see the container's port and the readiness probe settings. The user mentioned the readiness probe is http://:8080/healthz. Wait, the container might be listening on a different port. For example, if the app is running on port 9090 but the probe is checking 8080, that would fail. \n\nSo, using kubectl describe pod to get the container's port and the readiness probe details is the next step. That should clarify if there's a port mismatch or another issue with the probe. Let me call that command.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8485a27d0a459ea1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 227, 'prompt_tokens': 5922, 'total_tokens': 6149, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcf0-c290-7f33-8abf-3edf51611197-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8485a27d0a459ea1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5922, 'output_tokens': 227, 'total_tokens': 6149}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             user-service-746f96d8d6-fmppf\nNamespace:        social-network\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.31\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 17:54:45 +0000\nLabels:           app=user-service\n                  pod-template-hash=746f96d8d6\n                  service=user-service\nAnnotations:      <none>\nStatus:           Running\nIP:               10.244.0.120\nIPs:\n  IP:           10.244.0.120\nControlled By:  ReplicaSet/user-service-746f96d8d6\nContainers:\n  user-service:\n    Container ID:  docker://1ae46ecaa853533af27d26babdedfd8f1b2cb31755fdc744629d66fc97c3593e\n    Image:         docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\n    Image ID:      docker-pullable://deathstarbench/social-network-microservices@sha256:a845266e98ce15d204ee7b98d41a2a95820af525013ce70f14104f5b9b5e92b6\n    Port:          9090/TCP\n    Host Port:     0/TCP\n    Command:\n      UserService\n    State:          Running\n      Started:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 17:54:48 +0000\n    Ready:          False\n    Restart Count:  0\n    Read